a second attempt to evacuate civilians  from ukraine's southeastern city of  mariopol has failed residents were  expected to leave the city today during  a 10 am to 9 pm local ceasefire  ukrainian officials say evacuations were  stopped due to continued russian  shelling another attempt to evacuate  mario poll and another city besieged by  moscow's troops in southeastern ukraine  was also called off on saturday both  sides accused the other violating a  temporary truce some two hundred  thousand civilians are trapped in  mariupol which continues to be without  water and electricity and is fast  running out of food  let's cross to our correspondent nick  connolly who's in kiev nick let's start  with that situation in mario poll where  the cease-fire has been broken yet again  well it's an extraordinary drama going  on there in south country it's city  we've visited often big big city on the  sea of azov that is now surrounded on  three sides with only the sea open and  even there there are russian forces and  it is as you mentioned increasingly  humanitarian drama there no power  food stocks running down  no connection people not even able to  make sure their friends and family in  other parts of the country are safe or  get messages out  the  interpretation here in kiev the  ukrainian line would be that the  russians are just not interested in  letting the civilians out that this kind  of humanitarian disaster  puts more pressure on ukraine's  leadership to make compromise with  russia that russia has already basically  priced in the reputational damage that  this invasion of ukraine has done and is  basically going to play hardball i think  it's also important to remember that  it's extraordinarily difficult for these  two sides to reach agreements even on  more technical stuff like this at a time  where hundreds if not thousands of  soldiers on both sides are dying and you  know there is just extraordinary bad  blood i think without outside  intervention some kind of intermediary  from the red cross whatever i think it's  been very difficult for them to get any  kind of manageable deal on the on the  ground  a truly shocking situation now nick  you're in kiev what's the situation it  there in the capital and the surrounds  well as you might be able to see behind  me kiev is deserted we still a couple of  hours to go till the curfew but this is  a city that basically seems to be  abandoned it's not quite true there are  lots of people hiding behind the  curtains when you see them at the  supermarket you see them kind of peering  out occasionally but on the streets for  the most part it's journalists and  soldiers checkpoints everywhere sandbags  anti-tank uh kind of devices most of  them seemingly improvised everywhere  it's pretty shocking someone like me  who's lived here for four years to see  the city basically  expecting war this is a city that  obviously a country that has lived with  war in the east since 2014 when russia  annexed crimea and stoked those  separatist conflicts in the east but  that was always a long way away there  was always a night train ride away here  in kiev you could always be forgiven for  kind of thinking that this was just a  peaceful european capital and now russia  has brought that war to the streets of  kiev and everyone here very anxious  about you know the reports we've heard  of attempts by russia to try and kill  ukraine's democratically elected  president zielenski that there could be  sleeper agents operating and now  obviously russian troops within 20-25  kilometers of the capital even though  that advance has now seemingly got  stuffed some time there are shocking  pictures coming out of the northwestern  suburbs of kiev a place called european  butcher where there's been extraordinary  fighting humanitarian problems with  people trying to get out but railways  and roads shut it is a very eerie  situation here in here  nick over recent days we've been seeing  incredible pictures of men of all ages  queuing up to volunteer for the  ukrainian army in order to slow russia's  advance we're going to take a look now  at a report about what drives some of  those volunteers nick and we'll come  back to you after  these men are determined to fight for  their country  even in the face of russian firepower  and without support from nato  here in kiev they're waiting to sign up  for the ukrainian armed forces  resolved to do their part for the war  effort  it's my duty i want to be useful i want  to help i want to protect i want to  fight back i want my family to return to  ukraine as soon as possible and i will  do my best  other volunteers are stocking up for the  battle ahead and optimistic about  victory  we know why we defend our country and  our guys that are actually standing  there and fighting  our russian military forces they know  what they are doing those guys they  don't  we know what we are doing and that's why  we will win  it's that determination that's helped  inspire volunteers from much further  afield to join the fight ukraine  military  zalenski everything it's just  everything's been inspiring even like  the country's surrounding everybody's  just like it's like galvanized everybody  i've got no illusions i've got no  romantic ideas of war or i'm going to be  some hero or make a difference or any of  this but it's  it's what i do  i mean you could die here yes possibly  get willing to do it re-work  with heavy fighting already reaching the  city of european just outside kiev it  looks likely the volunteers bravery will  soon be put to the test  it's truly hard to imagine nick how  important are these volunteers for the  war effort and the ukrainian army  well it is obviously i think  first and foremost it's about morale  it's about showing these people are  staying and that they're not running  their way not trying to get abroad get  to safety and obviously for now i think  it's not so important in terms of the  manpower the ukrainian side if we  believe they're figures and obviously  there's always an element of uh pr in  terms of revealing your own losses as  opposed to the enemies but the ukrainian  side it seems seemingly hasn't lost as  many  men yet as the russian side but it's  about showing that there is that  willingness to sacrifice  even potentially their lives for the  country and a knowledge that if this  were to drag on which it looks set to  for now that there is the willingness  there to go out and fight ukraine for  now is more dependent on uh weapons  coming in from the west ammunition more  supplies they've also been asking for  air power for planes and also for  anti-aircraft systems because right now  russia is doing very badly in the land  war there are lots endless pictures on  social media have destroyed russian uh  tanks but also of fuel uh vehicles and  other bits of infrastructure and they're  seemingly not able to keep up with the  logistics and getting stuck often having  to abandon tanks for lack of fuel um so  that's what ukraine now is really asking  for air power anti-aircraft guns to  prevent russia  driving back it's down its advantage  from the air bombing cities bombing  military installations and that is the  big request now from the ukrainian  government to its western neighbors who  for now are you know keeping back from  that and not willing quite to go that  next step and potentially uh draw aya  from vladimir putin and the russian  leadership  dw's nick connolly reporting there from  kiev nick thanks very much  you
 

